[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Concurrency Control: Optimistic vs Pessimistic Locking (@Version) in JPA"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When multiple transactions attempt to update the same database record simultaneously, race conditions can occur. Java Persistence API (JPA) provides two primary strategies for managing data concurrency: Optimistic Locking and Pessimistic Locking. Choosing the right strategy is critical for application performance and data integrity."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Optimistic Locking and the @Version Annotation"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Optimistic locking assumes that multiple transactions can complete without interfering with each other. Data is not locked when read. Instead, JPA uses a version column (annotated with @Version) to detect if another transaction has modified the entity since it was last read. If a conflict occurs, an OptimisticLockException is thrown at commit time."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
public class Account {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    private String owner;

    private BigDecimal balance;

    @Version
    private Long version;

    // Constructors, getters, and setters
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Pessimistic Locking"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Pessimistic locking assumes that concurrent conflicts are likely and therefore locks the database records immediately upon reading them. Other transactions must wait until the lock is released. This approach prevents concurrent updates entirely, trading throughput and scalability for strict consistency."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"@Lock(LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE)
@Query("SELECT a FROM Account a WHERE a.id = :id")
Optional<Account> findByIdWithLock(@Param("id") Long id);"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Key Differences and When to Use Them"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Optimistic Locking: Best for read-heavy systems with low contention. It scales better because it holds no database locks, but requires handling transaction retries when conflicts occur."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pessimistic Locking: Best for write-heavy systems or critical financial operations where data collisions are unacceptable. It reduces concurrency and can introduce deadlocks if not managed properly."}]
